  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 9,
    "lesson_title": "Sums of Periodic Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine whether a sum of continuous-time periodic signals is periodic and find its repeating period by comparing component periods, angular frequencies, and harmonic relationships.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 9,
        "lesson_title": "Sums of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Sums of Periodic Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a sum $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$, the key question is whether one positive shift $T$ repeats the whole sum, not whether each component is periodic in isolation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the base case, start with two components that already share the same positive period $T$. That is the simplest situation because the same shift can be tested against both components at once.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_1(t+T)=x_1(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_2(t+T)=x_2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If both components satisfy those equalities for the same $T$, then the sum satisfies $x(t+T)=x(t)$ as well, because each term returns to itself under the same shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When the component periods differ, do not reuse one component period automatically. Later examples will search for a common repeating time by comparing periods directly, then angular frequencies and harmonic relationships, and they will also show cases where no shared repeat exists.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That shared-shift idea is the only tool this introduction needs: if a single positive shift repeats every component, it repeats the sum, and the rest of the lesson asks how to find or rule out that shared shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 9,
        "lesson_title": "Sums of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Sums of Signals with the Same Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine whether $x(t)=\\cos(2\\pi t)+\\sin(2\\pi t+\\pi/3)$ is periodic and, if so, identify a repeating period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is that both terms already repeat after the same positive shift. Because the coefficient of $t$ is $2\\pi$ in each component, each signal has period $T=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_1(t+1)=\\cos\\bigl(2\\pi(t+1)\\bigr)=\\cos(2\\pi t+2\\pi)=\\cos(2\\pi t)=x_1(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_2(t+1)=\\sin\\bigl(2\\pi(t+1)+\\pi/3\\bigr)=\\sin(2\\pi t+2\\pi+\\pi/3)=\\sin(2\\pi t+\\pi/3)=x_2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since both components return to themselves after the same shift, the sum does too: $x(t+1)=x_1(t+1)+x_2(t+1)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)=x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $x(t)$ is periodic, and one repeating period is $T=1$. The key point is that the shared period belongs to the whole sum because it belongs to every component.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 9,
        "lesson_title": "Sums of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding a Common Period from Component Periods",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine whether $x(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)+\\sin(2\\pi t/3)$ is periodic and, if so, find a repeating period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is that the component periods are different, so the goal is to find a common positive shift that repeats both terms at once. From the coefficients of $t$, $x_1(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)$ has period $2$ and $x_2(t)=\\sin(2\\pi t/3)$ has period $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_1=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\pi}=2,\\qquad T_2=\\frac{2\\pi}{2\\pi/3}=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first shared repeat time is the least common multiple of $2$ and $3$, so $T=6$. That is the smallest positive shift that can repeat both components together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_1(t+6)=\\cos\\bigl(\\pi(t+6)\\bigr)=\\cos(\\pi t+6\\pi)=\\cos(\\pi t)=x_1(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_2(t+6)=\\sin\\bigl(\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}(t+6)\\bigr)=\\sin\\bigl(\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}t+4\\pi\\bigr)=\\sin\\bigl(\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}t\\bigr)=x_2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since both components return to their original values after $6$, the sum does too: $x(t+6)=x_1(t+6)+x_2(t+6)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)=x(t)$. So $x(t)$ is periodic, and one repeating period is $T=6$; any larger common multiple also works, but $6$ is the least shared repeat.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 9,
        "lesson_title": "Sums of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding a Common Period from Component Angular Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine whether $x(t)=\\cos(4t)+\\sin(6t-\\pi/4)$ is periodic and, if so, find a repeating period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that both coefficients of $t$ are multiples of a smaller angular frequency. Here $4=2\\cdot 2$ and $6=3\\cdot 2$, so both components are harmonics of $2$ rad/s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4=2\\cdot 2,\\qquad 6=3\\cdot 2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A shared base angular frequency of $2$ rad/s corresponds to period $T=\\frac{2\\pi}{2}=\\pi$. So $\\pi$ is the candidate repeat for the sum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos\\bigl(4(t+\\pi)\\bigr) &= \\cos(4t+4\\pi) = \\cos(4t),\\\\\n\\sin\\bigl(6(t+\\pi)-\\pi/4\\bigr) &= \\sin(6t-\\pi/4+6\\pi) = \\sin(6t-\\pi/4)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because both terms return to themselves after the same shift, the sum does too: $x(t+\\pi)=x_1(t+\\pi)+x_2(t+\\pi)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)=x(t)$. So $x(t)$ is periodic, and one repeating period is $T=\\pi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 9,
        "lesson_title": "Sums of Periodic Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Sums with No Shared Repeating Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine whether $x(t)=\\cos(t)+\\cos(\\sqrt{2}t)$ is periodic.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that each term is periodic on its own, so the test is whether they share a positive repeat time. From the angular frequencies, $x_1(t)=\\cos(t)$ has period $T_1=2\\pi$ and $x_2(t)=\\cos(\\sqrt{2}t)$ has period $T_2=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\pi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_1=2\\pi,\\qquad T_2=\\sqrt{2}\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The ratio $T_1/T_2=\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational. An exact common repeat would have to be a common positive multiple of both periods, so it would require integers $m$ and $n$ with $2\\pi m=\\sqrt{2}\\pi n$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2\\pi m=\\sqrt{2}\\pi n \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 2m=\\sqrt{2}\\,n",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That would force $\\sqrt{2}=2m/n$, a rational number, which is impossible. So no positive common shift exists, and $x(t)$ is not periodic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
